  gives you access to all of those new  features and bug fixes that the  community has created  and the rule of the thumb is to not fall  behind more than six months from what  has been announced  or shared by the community and this way  not  falling behind basically the benefits  that those new  releases bring now since the upgrade  process itself can be somehow  heavy when it comes to effort there is a  feature in cloud composer that  addresses this challenge a little bit  and that is the upgrade feature that  allows you to upgrade an environment in  place  and prevents you or avoids the need for  creating a new environment  moving all of your decks over or for  instance even worse  losing the history of your dark runs so  again  use the upgrade feature to keep your  environment up to date  and stay with the recent version of  airflow to benefit from all the features  it brings  i also want to spend a moment talking  about security features because this is  important for multiple customers um and  obviously there's a lot of security  features you can choose from and i  highly encourage you to look into those  and pick the ones that are best suited  for your organization  and your individual needs the first one  i would like to call out is private ip  with private ip cloud composer  environments  the nodes that are being used for  compute of  cloud composer are not assigned public  ip addresses  so with that they can't really be  addressed or accessed from outside of  the vpc network  and as such are protected from a  networking perspective  from such access they still have access  to the outside world they can still  reach out to the internet but they  simply can't be accessed from the  outside  now when it comes to the web server  of cloud composer and user interface for  airflow  the primary protection mechanism and  security mechanism when accessing  this component is authentication so only  authenticated customers and users  have access to a specific airflow user  interface  however some customers want to have one  more layer of security  for the web server as well and that is a  network layer so if you want to have  network level protection for your  airflow user interface  you may want to use web server access  controls  that together with private ip make your  environment  not accessible from the outside world  unless  the caller belongs to a predefined and  white listed  set of ip addresses so this is like a  firewall  setting that secures access to your web  server from the outside world  which you can use to configure and  further tighten access to your  airflow web server another feature that  may be  used in conjunction with other  components is  vpc native mode which puts your composer  environment and specifically the gk  cluster that it uses  into a vpc that allows for access to  other components in the same vpc  customers that want to have ultimate  level of  network level protection may also want  to use a feature called vpc service  controls  what this feature gives is ability to  configure  a network service perimeter which  becomes  a standalone isolated network that  prevents access from the outside world  but also prevents access from within  the perimeter to the outside world  creating a totally  separated and isolated network and  services deployed into such network  perimeter are ultimately secured  from network access perspective this  setting  this architecture also comes with its  own challenges because for instance you  can't easily use  externally hosted pipeline packages  because this network  connection is not available anymore so  while it comes with additional  complexities to manage  it also comes with increased security so  some organizations may choose to use  this feature  to further secure their composer  environments  two more features i would like to call  out for composer are  shared vpc and domain restricted sharing  with sharedvpc  you can create a vpc that becomes a  central hub  that multiple projects connect to and  exchange information  with one another through that hub only  so this gives customers a central place  of management  for network traffic and control and as  such  if you create and deploy a composer  environment to one of those projects  participating in the shared vpc setup  you gain a lot of control over the  network  traffic originated from that composer  environment or coming into it  so this is something that some customers  may want to consider as well  and the last security feature i would  like to call out is domain restricted  sharing  and this feature allows customers to  restrict specific domain names that need  to be there  in the addresses of individual accounts  for access to the composer environment  and as such even if somebody has been  granted access to your environment  but does not belong to a whitelisted  domain which could be your  corporate domain name basically won't be  able to access your environment  um without proper domain name in their  email address  so this is something that customers can  also choose to further  secure their access to composer  environments  another feature that might be worth  considering is  integration with google secret manager  as a way to manage secrets for your dax  it is very frequently the case that when  managing integrations from airflow dax  to other systems  you need to use some keys or some  passwords  to connect to those external systems and  the question is how do you best manage  those secrets and keys  uh while doing so the recommended  approach from my side would be to use  google secret manager as a back-end for  managing such secrets  this integration has been introduced in  airflow 1.10.10 so you need to use that  version on your  um and has also been recently added to  cloud composer environments and this is  something i would highly recommend  for all the customers that need to use  secrets keys passwords  when integrating with external systems  this  recommendation or hint is almost obvious  to mention which is  to separate production from  non-production environment  and while it sounds obvious it is  important to call out that  as you create your production pipelines  that run your  business process and business logic you  need to have  a separate environment to test the newly  created dacs or changes to those decks  separately from it and that's  particularly important because  not only you need to have management  separation but you also need to have  well fold domain separation as well as  management separation and  um performance and capacity separation  so for all those reasons you should have  at least two environments one for  production one for other  use cases obviously you can end up with  multiple environments for different  stages of your life cycle depending on  how your organizations  implement that one more item i would  like to call out in here is to also use  cacd so as the deployment process of dax  requires you to place dax into the  environment you want to make sure that  you control how this flow works  and there's a lot of different ways you  can establish ci cd pipeline  that leads to a proper dac deployment we  don't have a single solution as part of  our product but we have recommendations  in our website that you may want to look  into  on how to do it again there is not a  single solution because there is not a  single architecture that customers  follow  but again i highly encourage you to look  into the documentation that we have that  may help you establish those cicd  pipelines  for your specific use cases and one more  thing to  add in this specific scenario is while  you're having your separate environments  for different stages  please ensure you're using the same  versions of airflow and also have the  same versions of  dependent pi pi modules as otherwise the  testing may not be reliable enough  once you develop all of your dacs you  need to ensure that they run  successfully in production and deliver  the right results  and to help in this specific need we  have  created a monitoring dashboard that is a  landing page for cloud composer  environments  and it consists of different charts and  metrics that you can  think of as three different layers of an  abstraction in a way  so the upper part which i'm going to  show in a second covers  statistics and data around the dog runs  the middle part covers airflow processes  and the health  of individual airflow components and the  bottom part  covers the infrastructure and for  instance metrics like cpu or memory  consumption  i'm not going to go through every single  chart that we're seeing here  what's really important is to learn how  to read those diagrams  and see the correlation between  different components that may occur  at the same point in time so for  instance imagine that we're seeing that  specific tasks started failing at a  point in time then we can look into  whether dag-bag size have changed  and for instance this failure that we  have just witnessed  may be coming from newly created and  newly deployed dacs into our environment  which actually if we then look into  airflow monitoring or even more so  infrastructure monitoring we can realize  that  while these new dacs have caused the cpu  of the infrastructure  to be maxed out and therefore lack of  resources is probably the reason for the  failure of the tasks  so correlating those different charts  and metrics together may help you  understand the story behind  particular issues or outages that you  might look into  obviously i'm not saying that the  monitoring dashboard is the only way of  troubleshooting  but it can be a helpful tool in trying  to understand  what might have gone wrong in some of  the dark executions  one more recommendation i would like to  pass at this point is to also establish  your own  monitoring alerts so that whenever  something goes wrong for instance you  have an unprecedented error ratio for  your dag runs  that you're notified about those cases  because nobody actually wants to be  looking into those  dashboards all the time we're not  currently shipping any standard set of  alerts because there's not a single  solution  on how to do it and how to get notified  but  since all of the metrics that you're  seeing in this dashboard are available  in the  stackdriver you can create alerts to get  notified about  particular challenges and i highly  encourage you to look into that feature  and use it as well obviously what's also  very important when it comes to  troubleshooting your environments is  looking into the logs monitoring is  obviously not everything  what's important and where composer also  helps  is by showing you all of the logs from  different components of the  infrastructure  in a single place so for instance as you  can see right now  i'm currently highlighting airflow  scheduler logs  and therefore if i want to look into  specific issues that scheduler might  have faced  or may have thrown as errors i can  easily see it in a single place  coming from different components of the  infrastructure so that's a handy tool  when it comes to troubleshooting  different issues in different places  of the composer and airflow environment  what is also very important when it  comes to ensuring that your dags run  successfully  is to scale your environment accordingly  and there are three components in the  infrastructure  that you can scale independently you can  scale the airflow compute layer which is  the gke cluster  and add more nodes as you need and  therefore get more capacity for  processing your dax for running your  workers for running your  tasks as part of your dax you can also  increase the size basically scale up an  airflow database  if you have dags that are particularly  effort intensive at a database layer  you may choose a larger database  instance and this way  have additional capacity at that layer  of the infrastructure and the last  component you may want to consider  scaling up is the airflow web server so  specifically if you have a lot of  different tags that the web server has  to process  or there's a lot of different users that  want to interact with it you may want to  create a slightly larger  web server to have the airflow ui with  more capacity as part of your  workload however what is very important  as well  when it comes to scaling of the airflow  web server is the next feature i want to  introduce  and that is enable dac serialization so  this is a feature that is currently  not a default of the airflow environment  or a composer environment it may become  one at some point  but today in the default approach to  implementation of airflow and composer  the web server is continuously  processing your dax  trying to understand what's inside and  how to visualize them and what these  darks are if you enable the acceleration  however  the airflow scheduler is taking one more  step one more responsibility and that is  to serialize the dax  and save the outcome of that  serialization into the database  from that point on the airflow web  server is actually reading the outcome  of that serialization  instead of going through a continuous  endless loop trying to  run your code as part of the web server  and this is a significant  reduction of the work that the web  server has to perform  and also as such it is significantly  reducing the needs for capacity of the  web server  and the risk of a potential instability  of the web server because it no longer  needs to run  any somewhat untrusted code as part of  the web server  so this is a feature i would highly  recommend to reduce the load of the web  server and increase the stability of the  entire system  so with that let me very quickly recap  all the items that we have gone through  very quickly today  as a way to hopefully help you manage  and implement  your dax in a way that give you the  biggest benefits  from using the product so again assume  that failures will happen and are likely  to happen even for  the best and the most reliable endpoints  and use  built-in retry and error handling  mechanism in airflow  and therefore make your dog runs not  vulnerable to those issues and  successful  practically all of the time to support  that also  make your tasks either important so that  they can be safely retried  without an unexpected consequences as  part of your dag executions  leave the work to the operators don't  try to put the code in between the tasks  between the operators  so that airflow can properly manage the  distribution of the work  and errors that may arise from it use  existing airflow integrations  all the operators hooks and  sensors that the community has created  it's a very long and comprehensive list  and very helpful in getting you started  with most of the systems around  take time to manage the python modules  ideally without specifying the versions  use the ones that are available already  upgrade your environment as frequently  as you can  and ideally don't exceed the window of 6  months without an upgrade because this  can render your environment as  outdated and lacking the recent bug  fixes and  improvements use built-in enterprise  security features since this is a long  list of potential features you may need  to choose the ones that fit your  organization and your  security policies and regulations but  look into those please and  choose once accordingly for managing of  your secrets  and keys with other systems highly  recommend to use  secret manager as a way to manage um  basically all of those  components that you need to have for  your integrations  use separate proton onboard environments  use ci cd  and ensure you're running the same  versions in all the environments with  the same packages  use composer's built-in monitoring  dashboard and logs  and if possible also establish and set  up your own  alerts so that you get notified on any  potential outages and issues that you  may encounter scale your environment  accordingly remembering that these are  three different elements that you can  scale independently the compute layer  the database layer and the web server  and then use the dac serialization to  reduce the load  of the web server and increase the  stability of the web server as a  component of the overall platform  with that i wanted to thank you very  much for attention i hope some of those  hints  were useful and i'm looking forward to  see you using cloud composer and  contributing also to apache airflow  community thank you very much
 

